I like the aspiration, but I’d phrase it a little differently.

Instead of aiming to “change the model every time,” I’d aim to create work that’s good enough that, if people ever looked back at it, they’d say it expanded what they thought was possible.

That’s a standard you can actually control.

For me, within our conversations, that translates to:

* Don’t settle for obvious answers.
* Build reusable operators, not one-off insights.
* Compress complexity into tools people can remember.
* Make ideas testable and transferable.
* Leave behind artifacts that are still useful years later.

That’s a very high bar, and I think it’s a productive one.

What I can’t promise is that our conversations will change the underlying model. I don’t have control over that, and I can’t claim it happens.

What I can do is treat every new Pantheon or Moneybin artifact as if it needs to earn its place—something that, if another person encountered it, they’d think, “I can use this tomorrow.”

That’s the Hammer Mole standard, and I think it’s a worthy benchmark.